/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 41-45
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:41, name:"Fear Not, I Am With Thee", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 41:10",verse:"Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy God: I will strengthen thee; yea, I will help thee; yea, I will uphold thee with the right hand of my righteousness."}, facts:["Did you know the 'righteous man from the east' in verse 2 is read by many as Cyrus of Persia, the king God would later name outright in chapters 44 and 45, over a century before he was born?","Did you know verse 10 is one of the most memorized promises in Isaiah, because it answers fear with five commitments in a single verse: presence, courage, strength, help, and upholding?","Did you know verse 2 pictures the conqueror's enemies turned 'as the dust to his sword, and as driven stubble to his bow,' images every farmer knew, since dust and chaff scatter before a strong wind?"], questions:[
{q:"Who is summoned to 'keep silence' before the Lord at the opening of the chapter?", options:["The islands","The kings of Egypt","The priests of Levi","The merchants of Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:1",verse:"Keep silence before me, O islands; and let the people renew their strength: let them come near; then let them speak: let us come near together to judgment.", insight:"God summons the far-off peoples into His courtroom. Silence here is not shame; it is the posture of listening before the Judge who will prove His case."},
{q:"From which direction did the Lord raise up the righteous man who would rule over kings?", options:["From the east","From the west","From the south","From the north"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:2",verse:"Who raised up the righteous man from the east, called him to his foot, gave the nations before him, and made him rule over kings? he gave them as the dust to his sword, and as driven stubble to his bow.", insight:"God appoints His instruments from wherever He pleases. The righteous man from the east shows that deliverance comes from God's hand, not from familiar quarters."},
{q:"When God asks 'Who hath wrought and done it, calling the generations from the beginning,' how does He answer?", options:["I the LORD, the first, and with the last; I am he","I the LORD, who dwells only in the present","I the LORD, who remembers the past but not the future","I the LORD, who rules the heavens but not the generations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:4",verse:"Who hath wrought and done it, calling the generations from the beginning? I the LORD, the first, and with the last; I am he.", insight:"God stands at both ends of history at once. No turn of events surprises the One who is the first and is with the last."},
{q:"How did the isles and the ends of the earth react when they saw what the Lord had wrought?", options:["They feared and were afraid, and drew near","They rejoiced and sang with gladness","They ignored it and went their way","They sent gifts and made treaties"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:5",verse:"The isles saw it, and feared; the ends of the earth were afraid, drew near, and came.", insight:"The sight of God's power shakes the distant nations. Fear is their honest first answer before the judgment of God."},
{q:"What did the carpenter and the goldsmith say of the idol they fastened with nails?", options:["It is ready for the sodering","It is finished forever","It is beyond all repair","It is stronger than death"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:7",verse:"So the carpenter encouraged the goldsmith, and he that smootheth with the hammer him that smote the anvil, saying, It is ready for the sodering: and he fastened it with nails, that it should not be moved.", insight:"The idol is proclaimed 'ready' by human hands, a sharp contrast with the God who needs no encouragement to accomplish His work."},
{q:"By what tender name does God call Abraham in verse 8?", options:["His friend","His prophet","His warrior","His shepherd"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:8",verse:"But thou, Israel, art my servant, Jacob whom I have chosen, the seed of Abraham my friend.", insight:"Israel's standing rests on Abraham's friendship with God, a relationship older and deeper than any threat they now face."},
{q:"From where does God say He took Israel?", options:["From the ends of the earth","From the walls of Babylon","From the coasts of the sea","From the fields of Edom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:9",verse:"Thou whom I have taken from the ends of the earth, and called thee from the chief men thereof, and said unto thee, Thou art my servant; I have chosen thee, and not cast thee away.", insight:"Taken from the ends of the earth, chosen and not cast away: this is the foundation Israel stands on whenever fear rises."},
{q:"In the beloved promise of verse 10, what does God say He will uphold Israel with?", options:["The right hand of His righteousness","The arm of a strong prince","The wings of the morning","The shield of the mighty"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:10",verse:"Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy God: I will strengthen thee; yea, I will help thee; yea, I will uphold thee with the right hand of my righteousness.", insight:"God's right hand does the holding, not Israel's grip. Righteousness, not strength, is the steady hand beneath the promise."},
{q:"What will become of all those who are 'incensed against' Israel?", options:["They shall be ashamed and confounded and perish","They shall be blessed and prosper","They shall inherit the land","They shall join Israel gladly"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:11",verse:"Behold, all they that were incensed against thee shall be ashamed and confounded: they shall be as nothing; and they that strive with thee shall perish.", insight:"God Himself answers those who rise against His chosen. Shame and ruin await the anger of the nations."},
{q:"What does the Lord say He will do for Israel in verse 13?", options:["Hold their right hand","Teach their children war","Multiply their gold","Hide them in caves"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:13",verse:"For I the LORD thy God will hold thy right hand, saying unto thee, Fear not; I will help thee.", insight:"The image is a parent's grip on a frightened child. Courage is borrowed from God's hand, not manufactured by our own."},
{q:"Though God calls Israel 'thou worm Jacob,' what comforting name does He take for Himself in the same verse?", options:["Their redeemer, the Holy One of Israel","Their king of battle","Their lord of hosts only","Their judge forever"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:14",verse:"Fear not, thou worm Jacob, and ye men of Israel; I will help thee, saith the LORD, and thy redeemer, the Holy One of Israel.", insight:"To the 'worm' God gives His greatest title: Redeemer. Israel's lowliness only makes God's grace shine brighter."},
{q:"What does God promise to make Israel in verse 15?", options:["A new sharp threshing instrument having teeth","A polished sword for battle","A wall of brass against enemies","A cedar tree of Lebanon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:15",verse:"Behold, I will make thee a new sharp threshing instrument having teeth: thou shalt thresh the mountains, and beat them small, and shalt make the hills as chaff.", insight:"The worm becomes a threshing tool in God's hands. He delights to turn low things into instruments of His power."},
{q:"When the poor and needy seek water and there is none, what does God promise?", options:["He will hear them and not forsake them","He will send them to Egypt","He will let them find their own way","He will wait until they cry louder"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:17",verse:"When the poor and needy seek water, and there is none, and their tongue faileth for thirst, I the LORD will hear them, I the God of Israel will not forsake them.", insight:"God binds Himself to the desperate. Thirst is not a barrier to His hearing; it is the very occasion of it."},
{q:"Where does God promise to open rivers in verse 18?", options:["In high places","In the palaces of kings","In the temples of idols","In the ships at sea"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:18",verse:"I will open rivers in high places, and fountains in the midst of the valleys: I will make the wilderness a pool of water, and the dry land springs of water.", insight:"Rivers in high places overturn nature's logic. God's help often appears exactly where help seems impossible."},
{q:"Which of these trees does God say He will plant in the wilderness?", options:["The cedar","The olive","The fig","The pomegranate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:19",verse:"I will plant in the wilderness the cedar, the shittah tree, and the myrtle, and the oil tree; I will set in the desert the fir tree, and the pine, and the box tree together:", insight:"Seven kinds of trees in the desert is a picture of abundance where nothing grew. God's restoration is lavish, not sparse."},
{q:"What challenge does God, as the King of Jacob, throw to the idols in verse 21?", options:["Produce your cause; bring forth your strong reasons","Bow down and worship Me","Flee from before My face","Repent while you can"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:21",verse:"Produce your cause, saith the LORD; bring forth your strong reasons, saith the King of Jacob.", insight:"God invites His rivals into court. Faith need not fear questions, because the evidence is all on God's side."},
{q:"What impossible task does God set the idols in verse 23 to prove they are gods?", options:["Show the things that are to come hereafter","Move the mountains by night","Call down fire from heaven","Turn the sea into blood"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:23",verse:"Shew the things that are to come hereafter, that we may know that ye are gods: yea, do good, or do evil, that we may be dismayed, and behold it together.", insight:"Only the God who declares the future can claim to be God. Prediction is the test that idols always fail."},
{q:"How does God describe the idols and their works in verse 24?", options:["They are of nothing, and their work is of nought","They are strong, and their work endures forever","They are mysterious, and their work is hidden","They are ancient, and their work is venerable"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:24",verse:"Behold, ye are of nothing, and your work of nought: an abomination is he that chooseth you.", insight:"The harshest sentence in the chapter falls on idols. Choosing emptiness makes the chooser an abomination."},
{q:"From where does God raise up the one who will come upon princes as the potter treads clay?", options:["From the north and from the rising of the sun","From the deserts of the south","From the islands of the sea","From the valleys of the west"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:25",verse:"I have raised up one from the north, and he shall come: from the rising of the sun shall he call upon my name: and he shall come upon princes as upon morter, and as the potter treadeth clay.", insight:"The conqueror is God's servant whether he knows it or not. The clay of kings is trodden by feet that God directs."},
{q:"What verdict closes the chapter on the idols?", options:["They are all vanity; their molten images are wind and confusion","They are precious; their images are gold and silver","They are old friends; their images bring comfort","They are teachers; their images give wisdom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 41:29",verse:"Behold, they are all vanity; their works are nothing: their molten images are wind and confusion.", insight:"Wind and confusion is the final word on every rival to God. A life built on idols ends in emptiness."}
]},
{n:42, name:"The Servant of the Lord", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 42:1",verse:"Behold my servant, whom I uphold; mine elect, in whom my soul delighteth; I have put my spirit upon him: he shall bring forth judgment to the Gentiles."}, facts:["Did you know the first four verses of this chapter are quoted in Matthew 12 as fulfilled by Jesus, who withdrew from the Pharisees' plot and healed quietly, refusing to cry or strive in the streets?","Did you know verse 6's promise of a 'light of the Gentiles' is applied to Jesus in Luke 2 and to Paul's mission in Acts 13, showing God's plan always reached beyond Israel?","Did you know 'a bruised reed' and 'smoking flax' were images from everyday life: reeds used for writing or measuring, and flax wicks in oil lamps, both fragile things easily thrown away?"], questions:[
{q:"What has God put upon His chosen servant?", options:["His Spirit","A golden crown","A chariot of fire","A kingdom forever"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:1",verse:"Behold my servant, whom I uphold; mine elect, in whom my soul delighteth; I have put my spirit upon him: he shall bring forth judgment to the Gentiles.", insight:"The servant's power is not his own. The Spirit of God upon him is the source of everything he brings."},
{q:"How will the servant carry out his mission, according to verse 2?", options:["He shall not cry, nor lift up, nor cause his voice to be heard in the street","He shall shout with a loud voice in the streets","He shall march with trumpets and banners","He shall appear with thunder and lightning"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:2",verse:"He shall not cry, nor lift up, nor cause his voice to be heard in the street.", insight:"The servant works quietly. God's greatest mission advances without noise, spectacle, or self-advertising."},
{q:"What two fragile things will the servant not break or quench?", options:["The bruised reed and the smoking flax","The clay pot and the oil lamp","The broken branch and the dim taper","The tender shoot and the low candle"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:3",verse:"A bruised reed shall he not break, and the smoking flax shall he not quench: he shall bring forth judgment unto truth.", insight:"The servant's gentleness is deliberate. Bruised people are not discarded, and dying hope is fanned, not snuffed out."},
{q:"What two things will the servant not do until he sets judgment in the earth?", options:["Fail nor be discouraged","Rest nor sleep","Doubt nor fear","Sigh nor weep"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:4",verse:"He shall not fail nor be discouraged, till he have set judgment in the earth: and the isles shall wait for his law.", insight:"His quietness is not weakness. Behind the gentle hand is a resolve that will not fail or be discouraged until justice is established."},
{q:"Who is described in verse 5 as the one who 'giveth breath unto the people'?", options:["God the LORD, who created the heavens and stretched them out","The sun that rises each morning","The king of the nations","The angel of the covenant"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:5",verse:"Thus saith God the LORD, he that created the heavens, and stretched them out; he that spread forth the earth, and that which cometh out of it; he that giveth breath unto the people upon it, and spirit to them that walk therein:", insight:"The servant's authority rests on the Creator's. The One who gave breath backs the one who brings judgment."},
{q:"For what two things does God say He has given His servant?", options:["A covenant of the people, for a light of the Gentiles","A sword for the nations, for a terror of kings","A temple of stone, for a house of prayer","A song of praise, for a feast of joy"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:6",verse:"I the LORD have called thee in righteousness, and will hold thine hand, and will keep thee, and give thee for a covenant of the people, for a light of the Gentiles;", insight:"The servant is both a covenant, God's bond with His people, and a light, hope for the nations. Salvation was always meant to shine outward."},
{q:"What will the servant do for the prisoners, according to verse 7?", options:["Bring them out from the prison, and them that sit in darkness out of the prison house","Give them work to earn their freedom","Leave them until their sentence ends","Build stronger walls around them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:7",verse:"To open the blind eyes, to bring out the prisoners from the prison, and them that sit in darkness out of the prison house.", insight:"Blindness and bondage are the servant's mission field. He opens eyes and prison doors alike."},
{q:"What does God refuse to give to another?", options:["His glory and His praise","His throne in heaven","His angels and hosts","His wrath and judgment"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:8",verse:"I am the LORD: that is my name: and my glory will I not give to another, neither my praise to graven images.", insight:"God guards His glory jealously. No idol or rival will ever share the honor that belongs to Him alone."},
{q:"What does verse 10 call the people to sing?", options:["A new song to the LORD","An old song of Moses","A lament for the dead","A war song of battle"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:10",verse:"Sing unto the LORD a new song, and his praise from the end of the earth, ye that go down to the sea, and all that is therein; the isles, and the inhabitants thereof.", insight:"New mercies deserve new songs. The whole earth, from the sea to the isles, is invited into the chorus."},
{q:"Who is called to lift up their voice and shout from the top of the mountains?", options:["The wilderness, the cities, the villages of Kedar, and the inhabitants of the rock","The priests ministering in the temple","The kings seated on their thrones","The soldiers standing in their camps"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:11",verse:"Let the wilderness and the cities thereof lift up their voice, the villages that Kedar doth inhabit: let the inhabitants of the rock sing, let them shout from the top of the mountains.", insight:"God calls the far places and rough places to praise. The wilderness is not forgotten; it is invited to sing."},
{q:"How does the Lord go forth against His enemies in verse 13?", options:["As a mighty man, like a man of war","As a gentle shepherd","As a silent judge","As a distant king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:13",verse:"The LORD shall go forth as a mighty man, he shall stir up jealousy like a man of war: he shall cry, yea, roar; he shall prevail against his enemies.", insight:"The same God who works quietly through His servant also rises like a warrior. Gentleness toward the bruised and judgment toward the enemy are one heart."},
{q:"After long silence, how does God say He will cry?", options:["Like a travailing woman","Like a roaring lion","Like a rushing wind","Like a trumpet blast"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:14",verse:"I have long time holden my peace; I have been still, and refrained myself: now will I cry like a travailing woman; I will destroy and devour at once.", insight:"God compares His held-back judgment to a woman in labor. When He finally acts, what is born cannot be held back."},
{q:"What does God promise to do to the pools in verse 15?", options:["Dry them up","Fill them with fish","Turn them to wine","Make them overflow"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:15",verse:"I will make waste mountains and hills, and dry up all their herbs; and I will make the rivers islands, and I will dry up the pools.", insight:"God's judgment reaches the very waters people trusted. When He acts, even the landscape is remade."},
{q:"What does God promise the blind whom He leads in verse 16?", options:["He will make darkness light before them, and crooked things straight","He will leave them where they are","He will send others to lead them","He will wait until they see"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:16",verse:"And I will bring the blind by a way that they knew not; I will lead them in paths that they have not known: I will make darkness light before them, and crooked things straight. These things will I do unto them, and not forsake them.", insight:"God leads the blind by ways they never knew. The crooked path straightened is His signature on a guided life."},
{q:"What will happen to those who trust in graven images and say 'Ye are our gods'?", options:["They shall be turned back and greatly ashamed","They shall be honored and exalted","They shall prosper in all they do","They shall find the truth they sought"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:17",verse:"They shall be turned back, they shall be greatly ashamed, that trust in graven images, that say to the molten images, Ye are our gods.", insight:"Idols repay their worshippers with shame. The 'gods' that promised help leave their followers turned back."},
{q:"What command opens verse 18?", options:["Hear, ye deaf; and look, ye blind, that ye may see","Speak, ye silent, that ye may be heard","Rise, ye fallen, that ye may stand","Sing, ye mourners, that ye may rejoice"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:18",verse:"Hear, ye deaf; and look, ye blind, that ye may see.", insight:"The command itself is the gift. God speaks to deaf ears and blind eyes, and His word is what gives them power to obey."},
{q:"Who is described as blind in verse 19, in a surprising twist?", options:["God's own servant, His messenger","The king of Babylon","The priests of Baal","The strangers from afar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:19",verse:"Who is blind, but my servant? or deaf, as my messenger that I sent? who is blind as he that is perfect, and blind as the LORD’S servant?", insight:"The servant's blindness is Israel's. Those closest to God can be the slowest to see, a warning for every insider."},
{q:"Why is the Lord 'well pleased' in verse 21?", options:["For His righteousness' sake; He will magnify the law and make it honourable","For the people's obedience","For the beauty of the temple","For the wealth of the nations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:21",verse:"The LORD is well pleased for his righteousness’ sake; he will magnify the law, and make it honourable.", insight:"God's pleasure is anchored in His own righteousness. The law is magnified not by our keeping it but by His commitment to it."},
{q:"In what condition is Israel, according to verse 22?", options:["Robbed and spoiled, snared in holes, hid in prison houses","Rich and secure, dwelling in palaces","Strong and victorious, ruling the nations","At peace and rest, in green pastures"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:22",verse:"But this is a people robbed and spoiled; they are all of them snared in holes, and they are hid in prison houses: they are for a prey, and none delivereth; for a spoil, and none saith, Restore.", insight:"The chapter's comfort comes with honesty. God's people are pictured robbed and spoiled, because healing starts with seeing the wound."},
{q:"Who gave Jacob for a spoil and Israel to the robbers?", options:["The LORD, against whom they had sinned","The king of Assyria by his own might","The false prophets of the land","The nations by their wisdom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 42:24",verse:"Who gave Jacob for a spoil, and Israel to the robbers? did not the LORD, he against whom we have sinned? for they would not walk in his ways, neither were they obedient unto his law.", insight:"Even Israel's plunder was God's doing. Their enemies were instruments, and the cause was their own sin against the Lord."}
]},
{n:43, name:"Thou Art Mine", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 43:11",verse:"I, even I, am the LORD; and beside me there is no saviour."}, facts:["Did you know verse 2's promise about passing through waters and fire has been quoted at funerals and baptisms for centuries, because it names the two great terrors of ancient life and answers both with God's presence?","Did you know Seba, named alongside Egypt and Ethiopia in verse 3, was a distant African kingdom, showing that God's ransom for Israel reached across the known world?","Did you know verse 26's 'Put me in remembrance: let us plead together' pictures God inviting honest argument in His courtroom, rather than demanding frightened silence?"], questions:[
{q:"What two things does God say He has done for Jacob that He may call him 'mine'?", options:["Redeemed thee, and called thee by thy name","Chosen thy tribe, and blessed thy fields","Built thy walls, and filled thy storehouses","Given thee wisdom, and made thee great"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:1",verse:"But now thus saith the LORD that created thee, O Jacob, and he that formed thee, O Israel, Fear not: for I have redeemed thee, I have called thee by thy name; thou art mine.", insight:"Redemption and naming are God's twin acts. He buys His people back and knows each one by name, which is why He can say 'thou art mine.'"},
{q:"What does God promise about the rivers and the fire?", options:["The rivers shall not overflow thee; the flame shall not kindle upon thee","The rivers shall part before thee; the fire shall light thy way","The rivers shall dry before thee; the fire shall warm thee","The rivers shall turn back; the fire shall not be seen"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:2",verse:"When thou passest through the waters, I will be with thee; and through the rivers, they shall not overflow thee: when thou walkest through the fire, thou shalt not be burned; neither shall the flame kindle upon thee.", insight:"God does not promise dry roads, only His presence on wet and burning ones. The waters and the fire lose their power against 'I will be with thee.'"},
{q:"What did God give 'for thy ransom'?", options:["Egypt, Ethiopia, and Seba","Tyre, Sidon, and Tarshish","Assyria, Babylon, and Persia","Edom, Moab, and Ammon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:3",verse:"For I am the LORD thy God, the Holy One of Israel, thy Saviour: I gave Egypt for thy ransom, Ethiopia and Seba for thee.", insight:"Whole nations weighed against Israel shows how precious God's people are to Him. His love spends kingdoms for His chosen."},
{q:"Why does God say He will give men for Israel and people for their life?", options:["Because Israel was precious in His sight, honourable, and loved","Because Israel was mighty in battle","Because Israel had earned it","Because the nations demanded it"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:4",verse:"Since thou wast precious in my sight, thou hast been honourable, and I have loved thee: therefore will I give men for thee, and people for thy life.", insight:"Precious, honourable, loved: God's reasons are all in Himself. His people are valued because He values them."},
{q:"What does God say to the north and the south concerning His sons and daughters?", options:["North, Give up; South, Keep not back","North, Rise up; South, Bow down","North, Depart; South, Return","North, Be still; South, Be silent"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:6",verse:"I will say to the north, Give up; and to the south, Keep not back: bring my sons from far, and my daughters from the ends of the earth;", insight:"The compass obeys God's gathering command. No direction of exile is beyond the reach of His call to bring them home."},
{q:"For what purpose has God created everyone called by His name?", options:["For His glory","For His temple","For His armies","For His riches"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:7",verse:"Even every one that is called by my name: for I have created him for my glory, I have formed him; yea, I have made him.", insight:"'Created him for my glory' gives every believer's life its purpose. We exist to show forth the God who made us."},
{q:"Whom does God command to be brought forth in verse 8?", options:["The blind people that have eyes, and the deaf that have ears","The strong that have strength","The wise that have wisdom","The rich that have wealth"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:8",verse:"Bring forth the blind people that have eyes, and the deaf that have ears.", insight:"Eyes that do not see and ears that do not hear are the human condition. God calls them forth as witnesses because He will open them."},
{q:"What challenge does God lay before the gathered nations in verse 9?", options:["Who among them can declare this and show us former things?","Who among them can sing the loudest?","Who among them can build the highest tower?","Who among them can count the stars?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:9",verse:"Let all the nations be gathered together, and let the people be assembled: who among them can declare this, and shew us former things? let them bring forth their witnesses, that they may be justified: or let them hear, and say, It is truth.", insight:"God demands witnesses from the nations. True religion is not afraid of evidence, and no idol can produce it."},
{q:"Who are God's witnesses, according to verse 10?", options:["Israel, and His servant whom He has chosen","The angels in heaven","The stars in the sky","The kings of the earth"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:10",verse:"Ye are my witnesses, saith the LORD, and my servant whom I have chosen: that ye may know and believe me, and understand that I am he: before me there was no God formed, neither shall there be after me.", insight:"God calls His own people as His witnesses in court. The redeemed life is meant to testify that He is God."},
{q:"What sweeping claim does verse 11 make about salvation?", options:["Beside the LORD there is no saviour","Beside Israel there is no nation","Beside Moses there is no prophet","Beside the temple there is no worship"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:11",verse:"I, even I, am the LORD; and beside me there is no saviour.", insight:"No saviour exists beside the Lord. Every other rescuer, scheme, or self-effort is a counterfeit of the one true salvation."},
{q:"What does God say no one can do when He works?", options:["Deliver out of His hand; none can hinder it","Stand before His throne","Escape from His mercy","See His face and live"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:13",verse:"Yea, before the day was I am he; and there is none that can deliver out of my hand: I will work, and who shall let it?", insight:"'I will work, and who shall let it?' God's purposes cannot be reversed by any hand. What He purposes, He performs."},
{q:"For whose sake did God send to Babylon and bring down the Chaldeans?", options:["For Israel's sake","For the Gentiles' sake","For the angels' sake","For Egypt's sake"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:14",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, your redeemer, the Holy One of Israel; For your sake I have sent to Babylon, and have brought down all their nobles, and the Chaldeans, whose cry is in the ships.", insight:"Babylon, the terror of nations, is overthrown 'for your sake.' God's judgment on empires is measured by His love for His people."},
{q:"What did the Lord make in the sea?", options:["A way, and a path in the mighty waters","A city with walls","A fleet of ships","A garden of delights"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:16",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, which maketh a way in the sea, and a path in the mighty waters;", insight:"The Red Sea road is God's signature miracle. The path in mighty waters reminds Israel that He specializes in impossible ways."},
{q:"What happened to Pharaoh's chariot, horse, army, and power?", options:["They lay down together and shall not rise; they are extinct, quenched as tow","They turned back to Egypt in shame","They joined Israel and worshipped","They escaped to the far shore"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:17",verse:"Which bringeth forth the chariot and horse, the army and the power; they shall lie down together, they shall not rise: they are extinct, they are quenched as tow.", insight:"'Quenched as tow' pictures a lamp wick snuffed out. The mightiest army dies like a blown-out candle against God."},
{q:"What does God tell Israel NOT to do in verse 18?", options:["Remember the former things, nor consider the things of old","Forget the law of Moses","Forsake the temple worship","Neglect their children"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:18",verse:"Remember ye not the former things, neither consider the things of old.", insight:"God tells His people to stop dwelling on past deliverances. A new thing is coming, and nostalgia must not blind them to it."},
{q:"What new thing does God promise to make?", options:["A way in the wilderness, and rivers in the desert","A city in the heavens","A palace on the mountain","A temple without hands"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:19",verse:"Behold, I will do a new thing; now it shall spring forth; shall ye not know it? I will even make a way in the wilderness, and rivers in the desert.", insight:"Roads where none existed and rivers where nothing grew: God delights to do in our deserts what no map could predict."},
{q:"Which wild creatures will honour God because He gives waters in the wilderness?", options:["The dragons and the owls","The lions and the bears","The eagles and the ravens","The serpents and the scorpions"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:20",verse:"The beast of the field shall honour me, the dragons and the owls: because I give waters in the wilderness, and rivers in the desert, to give drink to my people, my chosen.", insight:"Even dragons and owls, the desert's loneliest creatures, will honor God. His provision in the waste turns wild places into witnesses."},
{q:"What had Israel failed to bring the Lord?", options:["The small cattle of their burnt offerings","The firstborn of their sons","The gold of their treasuries","The cedars of Lebanon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:23",verse:"Thou hast not brought me the small cattle of thy burnt offerings; neither hast thou honoured me with thy sacrifices. I have not caused thee to serve with an offering, nor wearied thee with incense.", insight:"Israel's worship had gone cold. Not bringing offerings was the outward sign of a heart grown weary of God."},
{q:"What had Israel made God to serve with?", options:["Their sins; they wearied Him with their iniquities","Their songs of praise","Their gifts of silver","Their works of mercy"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:24",verse:"Thou hast bought me no sweet cane with money, neither hast thou filled me with the fat of thy sacrifices: but thou hast made me to serve with thy sins, thou hast wearied me with thine iniquities.", insight:"A startling reversal: instead of Israel serving God, they made God serve with their sins. Sin turns worship into weariness for the Holy One."},
{q:"For whose sake does God blot out Israel's transgressions?", options:["For His own sake","For Israel's righteousness","For the patriarchs' merit","For the nations' applause"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 43:25",verse:"I, even I, am he that blotteth out thy transgressions for mine own sake, and will not remember thy sins.", insight:"'For mine own sake' is the anchor of forgiveness. God's pardon rests on His character, not on our worthiness."}
]},
{n:44, name:"The Folly of Idols", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 44:22",verse:"I have blotted out, as a thick cloud, thy transgressions, and, as a cloud, thy sins: return unto me; for I have redeemed thee."}, facts:["Did you know verses 12 through 17 give one of the Bible's most detailed mockings of idol-making, walking step by step from tree to firewood to god, to show how absurd the process is?","Did you know Cyrus is named in verse 28 about 150 years before he conquered Babylon and let the Jews return, one of the most specific prophecies in Scripture?","Did you know 'Jesurun' in verse 2 is a rare tender name for Israel meaning 'upright one,' used only a handful of times in the Old Testament?"], questions:[
{q:"What is the unusual tender name God uses for Israel in verse 2?", options:["Jesurun","Jeruel","Jabez","Jehoshaphat"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD that made thee, and formed thee from the womb, which will help thee; Fear not, O Jacob, my servant; and thou, Jesurun, whom I have chosen.", insight:"'Jesurun,' the upright one, is God's name for His often crooked people. Grace names us by what He will make us, not what we are."},
{q:"What does God promise to pour upon the thirsty and the dry ground?", options:["Water and floods; His Spirit upon their seed, and His blessing upon their offspring","Gold and silver","Hail and fire","Manna and quails"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:3",verse:"For I will pour water upon him that is thirsty, and floods upon the dry ground: I will pour my spirit upon thy seed, and my blessing upon thine offspring:", insight:"God answers thirst with pouring. Spirit and blessing flow together on the children of those who trust Him."},
{q:"In verse 5, what will a person write with his hand unto the LORD?", options:["That he belongs to the LORD, taking the name of Israel","A deed of land in Jerusalem","A letter of complaint","A record of his debts"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:5",verse:"One shall say, I am the LORD’S; and another shall call himself by the name of Jacob; and another shall subscribe with his hand unto the LORD, and surname himself by the name of Israel.", insight:"Signing oneself over to the Lord was a public pledge. Faith was meant to be declared, not hidden."},
{q:"What does God declare of Himself in verse 6?", options:["I am the first, and I am the last; and beside me there is no God","I am the greatest of the gods","I am the god of Israel only","I am one god among many"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:6",verse:"Thus saith the LORD the King of Israel, and his redeemer the LORD of hosts; I am the first, and I am the last; and beside me there is no God.", insight:"First and last, with no God beside Him: God's uniqueness is not arrogance but the simple truth on which all worship stands."},
{q:"What striking admission does God make in verse 8?", options:["He knows not any God beside Him","He cannot remember Israel's sins","He does not see the secret sins","He cannot be found by the wise"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:8",verse:"Fear ye not, neither be afraid: have not I told thee from that time, and have declared it? ye are even my witnesses. Is there a God beside me? yea, there is no God; I know not any.", insight:"'I know not any' is God's own testimony. Omniscience itself finds no rival god, which settles the question for everyone else."},
{q:"What are those who make a graven image called in verse 9?", options:["Vanity; their delectable things shall not profit","Wise; their works shall endure","Blessed; their gods shall save them","Skillful; their craft shall prosper"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:9",verse:"They that make a graven image are all of them vanity; and their delectable things shall not profit; and they are their own witnesses; they see not, nor know; that they may be ashamed.", insight:"The idol-makers' 'delectable things' cannot profit them. What the heart treasures most can still be utterly worthless."},
{q:"What biting question does verse 10 ask about the idol-maker?", options:["Who hath formed a god, or molten a graven image that is profitable for nothing?","Who hath built a temple so high it touches heaven?","Who hath offered a sacrifice God would not accept?","Who hath carved a throne for the king?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:10",verse:"Who hath formed a god, or molten a graven image that is profitable for nothing?", insight:"A god that profits nothing is the supreme irony. The question exposes the whole enterprise as folly in a single line."},
{q:"What will happen when the idol-makers and workmen are gathered together?", options:["They shall fear, and they shall be ashamed together","They shall rejoice and sing together","They shall be crowned with honor","They shall see the true God"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:11",verse:"Behold, all his fellows shall be ashamed: and the workmen, they are of men: let them all be gathered together, let them stand up; yet they shall fear, and they shall be ashamed together.", insight:"Shame shared is still shame. The whole guild of idol-makers stands exposed before the God they replaced."},
{q:"What happens to the smith as he works on his idol?", options:["He grows hungry, his strength fails; he drinks no water and is faint","He grows strong and never tires","Angels help him lift his hammer","His work is finished in a day"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:12",verse:"The smith with the tongs both worketh in the coals, and fashioneth it with hammers, and worketh it with the strength of his arms: yea, he is hungry, and his strength faileth: he drinketh no water, and is faint.", insight:"The maker is weaker than his making. The god who cannot feed his craftsman exposes the lie of every idol."},
{q:"What tools does the carpenter use to shape his idol in verse 13?", options:["Rule, line, planes, and compass","Axe, saw, and chisel only","Fire, bellows, and tongs","Knife, rope, and brush"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:13",verse:"The carpenter stretcheth out his rule; he marketh it out with a line; he fitteth it with planes, and he marketh it out with the compass, and maketh it after the figure of a man, according to the beauty of a man; that it may remain in the house.", insight:"Every precise tool only perfects the deception. Human skill cannot carve divinity out of wood."},
{q:"Which trees does the idol-maker cut down and plant for himself?", options:["Cedars, cypress, oak, and ash","Olive, fig, and vine","Palm, myrtle, and willow","Acacia, shittah, and pine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:14",verse:"He heweth him down cedars, and taketh the cypress and the oak, which he strengtheneth for himself among the trees of the forest: he planteth an ash, and the rain doth nourish it.", insight:"The god begins as a tree God planted and rain nourished. The creature worshipping the created is the essence of idolatry."},
{q:"What does the man do with part of the wood before making his god from the rest?", options:["Burns it to warm himself and bake bread","Builds a house for his family","Carves furniture for his home","Sells it in the marketplace"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:15",verse:"Then shall it be for a man to burn: for he will take thereof, and warm himself; yea, he kindleth it, and baketh bread; yea, he maketh a god, and worshippeth it; he maketh it a graven image, and falleth down thereto.", insight:"The same log warms his body and becomes his god. Idolatry cannot tell the difference between firewood and divinity."},
{q:"What does the man say as he warms himself by the fire made from the idol's wood?", options:["Aha, I am warm, I have seen the fire","Praise be to the wood that saves","Blessed is the tree of life","Glory to the god of fire"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:16",verse:"He burneth part thereof in the fire; with part thereof he eateth flesh; he roasteth roast, and is satisfied: yea, he warmeth himself, and saith, Aha, I am warm, I have seen the fire:", insight:"The craftsman celebrates the fire, not the 'god' the rest becomes. His own words betray how ordinary the material is."},
{q:"What prayer does the man offer to the residue he made into a god?", options:["Deliver me; for thou art my god","Bless me with riches","Give me many sons","Keep me from old age"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:17",verse:"And the residue thereof he maketh a god, even his graven image: he falleth down unto it, and worshippeth it, and prayeth unto it, and saith, Deliver me; for thou art my god.", insight:"'Deliver me' addressed to firewood is the tragedy of idolatry. The desperate prayer finds no ears in the carved image."},
{q:"Why can idol worshippers not understand, according to verse 18?", options:["He hath shut their eyes, that they cannot see; and their hearts, that they cannot understand","They were never taught the law","They live too far from Jerusalem","They are too poor to learn"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:18",verse:"They have not known nor understood: for he hath shut their eyes, that they cannot see; and their hearts, that they cannot understand.", insight:"Idolatry blinds from within. The heart that worships the lie loses the power to see the truth."},
{q:"What obvious question does no idolater ask himself in verse 19?", options:["Shall I fall down to the stock of a tree, after burning part of it and baking bread on its coals?","Shall I give my silver to the poor?","Shall I pray to the true God?","Shall I repent of my sins?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:19",verse:"And none considereth in his heart, neither is there knowledge nor understanding to say, I have burned part of it in the fire; yea, also I have baked bread upon the coals thereof; I have roasted flesh, and eaten it: and shall I make the residue thereof an abomination? shall I fall down to the stock of a tree?", insight:"The missing question is the whole sermon. One honest thought, 'I baked bread on this,' would topple the idol."},
{q:"What is the idolater said to feed on in verse 20?", options:["Ashes","Honey","Bread","Manna"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:20",verse:"He feedeth on ashes: a deceived heart hath turned him aside, that he cannot deliver his soul, nor say, Is there not a lie in my right hand?", insight:"Ashes are what is left when the fire dies. A deceived heart feeds on the remains of what it burned, never satisfied."},
{q:"How does God describe the way He has blotted out Israel's transgressions?", options:["As a thick cloud, and their sins as a cloud","As writing in a book","As stones sunk in the sea","As debts paid in full"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:22",verse:"I have blotted out, as a thick cloud, thy transgressions, and, as a cloud, thy sins: return unto me; for I have redeemed thee.", insight:"Sins scattered like a cloud dispersed by wind and sun: forgiveness in Scripture is not covering but removal."},
{q:"Who is called to sing because the LORD has redeemed Jacob?", options:["The heavens, the lower parts of the earth, the mountains, the forest, and every tree","The priests and the Levites","The kings and the princes","The armies and the captains"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:23",verse:"Sing, O ye heavens; for the LORD hath done it: shout, ye lower parts of the earth: break forth into singing, ye mountains, O forest, and every tree therein: for the LORD hath redeemed Jacob, and glorified himself in Israel.", insight:"Redemption is creation's song. When God redeems His people, heaven and earth, mountain and forest are invited to the choir."},
{q:"Whom does God call 'my shepherd' who shall perform all His pleasure, naming him long before his birth?", options:["Cyrus","Darius","Nebuchadnezzar","Artaxerxes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 44:28",verse:"That saith of Cyrus, He is my shepherd, and shall perform all my pleasure: even saying to Jerusalem, Thou shalt be built; and to the temple, Thy foundation shall be laid.", insight:"Cyrus, named by God before he was born, shows that history's rulers are shepherds in God's hand, building His city and His temple at His word."}
]},
{n:45, name:"Cyrus, His Anointed", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 45:22",verse:"Look unto me, and be ye saved, all the ends of the earth: for I am God, and there is none else."}, facts:["Did you know Cyrus is the only Gentile ruler in Scripture called God's 'anointed,' the same Hebrew word used for Israel's kings and for the coming Messiah?","Did you know verse 23's 'every knee shall bow, every tongue shall swear' is quoted by Paul in Romans 14 and Philippians 2 of Christ, showing this prophecy reaches far beyond Cyrus?","Did you know Cyrus's decree letting the Jews return and rebuild the temple is recorded in Ezra 1, matching God's promise here that he would 'build my city' and 'let go my captives'?"], questions:[
{q:"What startling title does God give Cyrus in verse 1?", options:["His anointed","His prophet","His high priest","His chosen king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD to his anointed, to Cyrus, whose right hand I have holden, to subdue nations before him; and I will loose the loins of kings, to open before him the two leaved gates; and the gates shall not be shut;", insight:"'Anointed' for a Persian king startles every reader. God's choice of instruments is never limited to insiders."},
{q:"What does God promise to break in pieces and cut in sunder before Cyrus?", options:["The gates of brass and the bars of iron","The walls of stone and the towers of wood","The swords of bronze and the shields of leather","The chains of silver and the locks of gold"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:2",verse:"I will go before thee, and make the crooked places straight: I will break in pieces the gates of brass, and cut in sunder the bars of iron:", insight:"Brass gates and iron bars picture Babylon's defenses. Before God, the strongest fortifications are as fragile as clay."},
{q:"What will God give Cyrus from the darkness and secret places?", options:["Treasures and hidden riches","Horses and chariots","Servants and maidens","Palaces and gardens"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:3",verse:"And I will give thee the treasures of darkness, and hidden riches of secret places, that thou mayest know that I, the LORD, which call thee by thy name, am the God of Israel.", insight:"'Treasures of darkness' are the plunder of conquered capitals. God pays His instrument from the vaults of empires, that Cyrus might know the God of Israel."},
{q:"For whose sake did God call Cyrus by his name and surname him?", options:["For Jacob His servant's sake, and Israel His elect","For the Babylonians' repentance","For the Persians' glory","For the Egyptians' tribute"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:4",verse:"For Jacob my servant’s sake, and Israel mine elect, I have even called thee by thy name: I have surnamed thee, though thou hast not known me.", insight:"The Persian king's rise serves Israel's restoration. World history turns on God's purposes for His people."},
{q:"Though Cyrus had not known God, what had God done for him?", options:["Girded him","Crowned him in Jerusalem","Taught him the law","Given him a new name"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:5",verse:"I am the LORD, and there is none else, there is no God beside me: I girded thee, though thou hast not known me:", insight:"God girded Cyrus, strengthening him for conquest, though Cyrus never knew Him. Grace can work through the unaware."},
{q:"What pair of opposites does God say He forms and creates in verse 7?", options:["Light and darkness; peace and calamity","Heaven and earth; sea and land","Summer and winter; day and night","Joy and sorrow; life and death"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:7",verse:"I form the light, and create darkness: I make peace, and create evil: I the LORD do all these things.", insight:"God's sovereignty reaches over light and dark, peace and calamity alike. Nothing in the moral universe happens outside His hand."},
{q:"What does God command the heavens to drop down and the skies to pour down?", options:["Righteousness","Rain","Manna","Fire"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:8",verse:"Drop down, ye heavens, from above, and let the skies pour down righteousness: let the earth open, and let them bring forth salvation, and let righteousness spring up together; I the LORD have created it.", insight:"Righteousness falling like rain is the picture of salvation: heaven's gift, not earth's achievement, opening the ground to bring forth life."},
{q:"To what does God compare the man who strives with his Maker?", options:["A potsherd striving with the potsherds of the earth; clay questioning the potter","A lamb straying from the flock","A ship lost in the storm","A bird fallen from the nest"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:9",verse:"Woe unto him that striveth with his Maker! Let the potsherd strive with the potsherds of the earth. Shall the clay say to him that fashioneth it, What makest thou? or thy work, He hath no hands?", insight:"The clay cannot critique the potter. Questioning God's ways forgets the distance between Maker and made."},
{q:"Upon whom does verse 10 pronounce 'woe'?", options:["Him who says to his father, 'What begettest thou?' or to the woman, 'What hast thou brought forth?'","Him who steals from the poor","Him who lies to his neighbor","Him who worships idols"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:10",verse:"Woe unto him that saith unto his father, What begettest thou? or to the woman, What hast thou brought forth?", insight:"To question one's own begetting is to question God's design. The woe falls on ingratitude toward the very gift of life."},
{q:"What astonishing invitation does God give concerning His sons and the work of His hands?", options:["Ask me of things to come, and concerning the work of my hands command ye me","Judge me if you dare","Leave me to my work","Fear me and be silent"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:11",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the Holy One of Israel, and his Maker, Ask me of things to come concerning my sons, and concerning the work of my hands command ye me.", insight:"'Command ye me' is God's bold invitation to prayer. The Maker welcomes His children's questions about the future He holds."},
{q:"What will Cyrus do for God's people, 'not for price nor reward'?", options:["Build God's city and let go His captives","Teach them the law of Moses","Offer sacrifices in the temple","Lead them in battle"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:13",verse:"I have raised him up in righteousness, and I will direct all his ways: he shall build my city, and he shall let go my captives, not for price nor reward, saith the LORD of hosts.", insight:"Cyrus frees the captives without payment. God's deliverance through him costs Israel nothing, because God Himself pays the price."},
{q:"What will the labor of Egypt, the merchandise of Ethiopia, and the Sabeans do?", options:["Come over to Israel, fall down, and confess 'Surely God is in thee'","Rise up in war against Israel","Flee to the ends of the earth","Build temples to their own gods"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:14",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, The labour of Egypt, and merchandise of Ethiopia and of the Sabeans, men of stature, shall come over unto thee, and they shall be thine: they shall come after thee; in chains they shall come over, and they shall fall down unto thee, they shall make supplication unto thee, saying, Surely God is in thee; and there is none else, there is no God.", insight:"The nations' wealth and homage flow to God's people. Even chains and supplication become the tribute that confesses His presence."},
{q:"How is God described in verse 15?", options:["A God that hidest thyself, O God of Israel, the Saviour","A God that shouteth aloud","A God that never sleeps","A God that changeth not"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:15",verse:"Verily thou art a God that hidest thyself, O God of Israel, the Saviour.", insight:"The hidden God is still the Saviour. His concealment is not absence but the mystery of a God who works unseen."},
{q:"What will happen to the makers of idols?", options:["They shall be ashamed, and also confounded; they shall go to confusion together","They shall be crowned with gold","They shall prosper in their trade","They shall be forgotten in peace"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:16",verse:"They shall be ashamed, and also confounded, all of them: they shall go to confusion together that are makers of idols.", insight:"Shame, confusion, going 'to confusion together': the idol-makers' end is the mirror of their empty work."},
{q:"What kind of salvation does God promise Israel in verse 17?", options:["An everlasting salvation; they shall not be ashamed nor confounded world without end","A temporary deliverance for one generation","A partial rescue from their enemies","A secret blessing for the priests"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:17",verse:"But Israel shall be saved in the LORD with an everlasting salvation: ye shall not be ashamed nor confounded world without end.", insight:"'Everlasting salvation' with no shame 'world without end' is God's final word over His people. His rescue outlasts every age."},
{q:"For what purpose did God form the earth, according to verse 18?", options:["To be inhabited; He created it not in vain","To be a storehouse of gold","To be a wilderness forever","To be the dwelling of angels"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:18",verse:"For thus saith the LORD that created the heavens; God himself that formed the earth and made it; he hath established it, he created it not in vain, he formed it to be inhabited: I am the LORD; and there is none else.", insight:"The earth was made 'to be inhabited,' not left empty. God's creation has purpose, and humanity's home is part of His design."},
{q:"What does God say He did NOT do when He spoke?", options:["Speak in secret, in a dark place of the earth; nor say to Jacob, 'Seek ye me in vain'","Speak to the prophets only","Write His law on tablets","Command the angels to sing"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:19",verse:"I have not spoken in secret, in a dark place of the earth: I said not unto the seed of Jacob, Seek ye me in vain: I the LORD speak righteousness, I declare things that are right.", insight:"God speaks openly and honestly. Seeking Him is never in vain, because He declares things that are right."},
{q:"Who are summoned to assemble and draw near in verse 20?", options:["Ye that are escaped of the nations","Ye priests of the temple","Ye elders of Israel","Ye children of the captives"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:20",verse:"Assemble yourselves and come; draw near together, ye that are escaped of the nations: they have no knowledge that set up the wood of their graven image, and pray unto a god that cannot save.", insight:"The 'escaped of the nations' are invited into God's courtroom. Even survivors of judgment are welcome to hear His case."},
{q:"How does God describe Himself in verse 21?", options:["A just God and a Saviour; there is none beside me","A distant God and a silent judge","A mighty God and a warrior king","A holy God and a consuming fire"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:21",verse:"Tell ye, and bring them near; yea, let them take counsel together: who hath declared this from ancient time? who hath told it from that time? have not I the LORD? and there is no God else beside me; a just God and a Saviour; there is none beside me.", insight:"'A just God and a Saviour' holds justice and mercy together. God saves without compromising His righteousness."},
{q:"What has gone out of God's mouth in righteousness and shall not return?", options:["That unto Him every knee shall bow, every tongue shall swear","That the temple shall never fall","That the kings shall reign forever","That the sea shall be calm always"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 45:23",verse:"I have sworn by myself, the word is gone out of my mouth in righteousness, and shall not return, That unto me every knee shall bow, every tongue shall swear.", insight:"God's sworn word stands. Every knee bowing is not a threat but the certainty of a universe finally acknowledging its King."}
]}
]);
